= Network =

== Overview ==
A '''network''' is a set of entities connected by relationships or interactions.

Networks are used to represent and study relational structure in biological, social, technological, informational, and physical systems.

== Nodes and links ==
A network model commonly contains:

* nodes or vertices representing entities;
* edges or links representing relations/interactions.

Links can be directed or undirected, weighted or unweighted, signed or unsigned, static or time-varying.

== Network and graph ==
A graph is a mathematical node–edge structure.

A network is often represented by a graph, and the words are frequently used interchangeably.

However, empirical networks can also include domain semantics, dynamics, uncertainty, multiple relation types, and measurement assumptions.

Thus a graph is a common formal representation of a network rather than necessarily the whole empirical system.

== Network properties ==
Network science studies properties such as:

* degree;
* path length;
* clustering;
* centrality;
* communities;
* connectivity;
* motifs;
* modularity;
* robustness;
* spreading dynamics.

== Computer networks ==
A [[Computer network]] is a technological network in which computing devices communicate.

The general concept of network is much broader.

== Knowledge and semantic networks ==
[[Knowledge graph|Knowledge graphs]] and [[Semantic network|semantic networks]] represent entities/concepts and their relations.

They are special types of informational network.

== Related concepts ==
[[Complex system]], [[Computer network]], [[Distributed system]], [[Knowledge graph]], [[Semantic network]], [[Robustness]], and [[System boundary]] provide the relational context.

